<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basavakalyan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalyani (Karnataka)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basavakalyan, historically Kalyani, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> from the mid-eleventh century and later of the Kalachuris of Kalyani, and in the twelfth century it was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, after whom it was renamed. Its fort and ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basavakalyan.jpg" alt="Basavakalyan" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sscheral · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basava_Gaint_Statue_108_feet,_Basava_Kalyana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
